From 540b3b545d55e27944e6ec1434d5cd5094211c8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 13:55:26 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 30/30] Add VSX and AltiVec row functions for libyuv on PowerPC

libyuv is the only YUV conversion and scaling path on PowerPC (gfx/ycbcr's
own rows are x86-only), so AVIF decoding, canvas and WebGL video uploads,
alpha video and WebCodecs all ran its scalar C rows. This adds the PowerPC
port of libyuv: CPU detection for VMX, VSX, POWER9 and POWER10; VSX rows for
the YUV to ARGB conversions (I420, I422 and I444 with and without alpha,
I400 and J400, NV12 and NV21, and the 10- and 12-bit I210, I410, P210 and
P410), ARGB attenuate and unattenuate, the matrix ARGB and RGB to Y and UV
rows, the packed RGB formats, the mirror and shuffle rows, the 8- and 16-bit
conversions, InterpolateRow and the planar, UV and ARGB scale rows; plus
AltiVec versions of the common rows for the PowerPC 970. The VSX rows are
gated on VSX and POWER8 on both byte orders, with the few endian-sensitive
lane tricks behind helpers. The new files are listed in libyuv.gypi and kept
out of the unified build in media/libyuv/moz.build.

Measured at 1080p on POWER9 in libyuv's own benchmark, I420 to ARGB is about
2.8x faster than the C rows, I444 3.9x, NV12 2.8x, ARGB attenuate 2.2x and
the ARGB scale rows 1.1x to 3.9x. The port tracks the libyuv revision that
160 vendors (9456b062).
